<section class="shell band">
  <h1>Retreats and training for therapists</h1>
  <div class="answer">
    <p>Reem Glasco, LPC-S, runs mindful trail walk retreats for interns, LPC Associates and
    licensed therapists, equine-assisted sessions and training near Dripping Springs, Texas,
    and periodic ethics continuing education for Texas LPCs. Equine-assisted supervision
    sessions are $60 each for her own associates. Retreats and training carry their own fees,
    set per offering.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="cta-row">
    <a class="cta" href="/contact/">Ask about the next one</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band--night">
  <div class="shell">
    <h2>Why any of this, when you already have supervision</h2>
    <p>Because the thing that limits most clinicians is not knowledge. It is how much they can
    stay present for before something in them takes over: the urge to fix, to reassure, to
    change the subject, to work harder than the client.</p>
    <p>That capacity does not grow much by talking about it. It grows by being in a situation
    where your own state is the material, and then noticing what you do. Walking does some of
    that. Horses do a lot of it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

