<section class="shell band">
  <h2>Ethics CEUs for Texas LPCs</h2>
  <p>Texas LPCs need 24 continuing education hours in each two-year renewal period, including at
  least 6 hours in ethics, and since January 2024 at least half of the total must come from a
  provider listed in §681.140(f). Reem offers ethics continuing education periodically:
  <a href="/for-therapists/ethics-ceus/">what is on offer</a>.</p>
  <p><a href="/texas-lpc-licensure/">The full renewal requirements are in the licensure
  guide</a>, including what LPC-S status adds.</p>
</section>

<section class="shell band faq">
  <h2>Questions</h2>

  <details>
    <summary>Do I have to be Reem's supervisee to attend a retreat?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>No. Associates in supervision with Reem are included, and other interns, associates and
      licensed therapists are welcome as space allows.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>How much do the equine sessions cost?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>$60 per session for LPC Associates in supervision with Reem, added on to the $425
      monthly supervision fee. Training for therapists who want to work with horses in their own
      practice is priced separately per offering.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Do the ethics CEUs count toward Texas LPC renewal?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Yes. Ethics hours count toward the 6-hour ethics requirement inside your 24 hours per
      renewal period, and they also fill the approved-provider half: at least half your total
      hours must come from a provider listed in 22 TAC §681.140(f), and subsection (f)(6) lists
      a licensee with supervisor status. Reem is an LPC Supervisor in Texas.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Is any of this therapy?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>No. Retreats and training are professional development, and supervision is supervision.
      If what you need is your own therapy, that is a good thing to want, and Reem can point you
      to a referral rather than mixing the roles.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>
